WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:02.008
Does the world need to rapidly

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.199
convert to using renewable energy

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.119
to save the planet from global

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:07.769
warming? That was the topic of a

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:09.599
Soho forum debate held at

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.459
the Sheen Center in New York City

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.499
on Monday, August 15th,

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:14.789
2022.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:16.619
Andrew Dessler, the director of the

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.449
Texas Center for Climate Studies at

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.399
Texas A&amp;M University, argued that

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.589
fossil fuels are endangering life

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:24.299
on the planet by causing global

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.919
warming through greenhouse gas

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.899
emissions. He contended that solar

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.119
and wind represent a safe,

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.188
reliable and cost effective means

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:34.169
to decarbonize the electric

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:35.999
grid. Steven Koonin, who served

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:37.949
as Under Secretary for science

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.419
at the Department of Energy during

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:41.189
the Obama administration, argued

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.809
that making large and rapid

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.459
reductions in greenhouse gas

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:46.319
emissions aren't necessary to

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:47.489
protect the earth.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:49.349
He also contended that doing so

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:51.209
isn't cost effective and that

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.019
it's immoral.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.639
The debate was moderated by Soho

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.289
Forum director Gene Epstein.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.229
The affirmative resolution reads

00:01:01.230 --> 00:01:03.509
Climate science compels

00:01:03.510 --> 00:01:05.639
us to make large and rapid

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.859
reductions in climate emissions.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.049
Andrew Dessler for the affirmative.

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:11.849
Andrew, please come to the stage.

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.509
Speaking for the negative.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:24.769
Steven Koonin.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.360
Steven, please come to the stage.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.809
Jane, please close

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:37.459
the voting.

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.319
Andrew, you have 17

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:40.549
and half minutes to speak for the

00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:41.419
resolution.

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:42.420
Take it away, Andrew.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:47.369
Thank you.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.869
And if you have a crinkly lossage,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:49.679
it won't bother me.

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.479
So feel free to pop it out.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:52.769
You see my slides?

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.389
Okay, so as I was saying, this is

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.339
30,000 years

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.379
of or 30,000 years

00:01:58.380 --> 00:02:00.239
of history are the last 20,000, the

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:01.559
next 10,000. This is the last

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.909
glacial maximum.

00:02:02.910 --> 00:02:04.199
So that's dinosaurs are not

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:05.609
dinosaurs. That's ice sheets,

00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.589
mastodons,

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.448
ice, thousands of feet thick

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:10.948
over North America.

00:02:10.949 --> 00:02:12.659
And then you warm up out of it into

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:13.469
their glacial.

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:15.179
And then this pink band is the

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.069
fossil fuel era

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:19.109
in the long span of Earth's

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:19.949
history.

00:02:19.950 --> 00:02:21.899
Fossil fuels can be this brief

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.759
spark in our history.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.829
So it's only a couple hundred years,

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.059
you know, maybe we'll stop burning

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.649
fossil fuels in a few decades, maybe

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.729
in a few centuries.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:30.779
But we're not going to be burning

00:02:30.780 --> 00:02:32.039
fossil fuels in a few thousand

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.539
years. Just really aren't enough

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:34.769
recoverable fossil fuel.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.569
So it's a couple of hundred years

00:02:36.570 --> 00:02:37.799
and you see the temperature is going

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.839
to spike and then emissions

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.309
will end and the temperature will

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:42.539
not cool off. Very quickly, I'll

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.129
take 100,000 years for temperatures

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:46.589
to come back down. So what we do

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:48.479
during this pink band is

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:50.369
going to be irreversible,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.179
basically.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:53.279
Now, my argument here

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.589
is that this is a tremendous

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:56.849
amount of warming that should scare

00:02:56.850 --> 00:02:58.619
the crap out of you.

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.149
All right. Now, let me back in.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:01.979
They explain why I think

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:02.980
that.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.879
So the difference between an ice

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:07.919
age and interglacial is ten

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.449
degrees Fahrenheit.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:11.279
And there's really no no dispute

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:12.359
about this number. Lots of people

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:13.469
have worked on it. We really

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:14.969
understand it. If you cool the

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:16.859
planet by ten degrees Fahrenheit

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:18.569
global average, you end up with

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.609
thousands of feet of ice over North

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.469
America, sea levels 300 feet

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:23.399
lower.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:24.749
Now, if you look at what we might

00:03:24.750 --> 00:03:27.089
do, six degrees.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.829
60% of an ice age.

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:30.299
Now, it's not in the same direction.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:31.559
We're not cooling towards an ice

00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:33.209
age. We are warming past an ice age

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:35.039
and it's going to remake the

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:35.819
planet.

00:03:35.820 --> 00:03:37.379
And many of you, I see a lot of

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.059
young people in the audience.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.219
You're going to live through this.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.779
Okay. So you have skin in the game?

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:43.889
I don't have as much skin in the

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.999
game. I don't plan on living through

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:46.919
the 21st century, but my kids

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.559
do and a lot of you all do

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.369
now.

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.259
So this is enough to basically

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.109
re shape the planet

00:03:54.110 --> 00:03:55.649
when you walk out your front door

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.779
and 2100 a six degree warmer planet,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.669
you're not going to recognize it.

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:01.529
So how do we know

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:02.429
this? Well, first of all, we've

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:04.259
already warmed about two degrees.

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.669
This black dot is where we are now.

00:04:05.670 --> 00:04:06.719
This is a little ice age.

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:08.399
We're about two degrees above that.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.679
And we can already see

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:12.929
extremely big impacts

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.119
just from two degrees.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.099
One third of the ultimate warming.

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.328
Oh, I should say when I talk about

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:19.979
six degrees, that's kind of a

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:21.208
business as usual.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:22.769
If we decide we want to burn lots of

00:04:22.770 --> 00:04:23.939
fossil fuels, it's going to be more

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:26.399
than that. And if we're wise,

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.899
it can be a lot less than that.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.429
That's and that's essentially what

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:30.479
we're trying to decide in this

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.369
debate. Now, this is what we already

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:33.449
see with two degrees.

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.359
You know, you see the U.S.,

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.229
Southwest Lake Mi, Lake Powell

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:40.559
is running out of water, literally

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.479
a Hurricane Harvey, an

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:43.379
unbelievable.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.879
I lived through that in College

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:46.379
Station about 90 miles from Houston.

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:47.309
I can tell you it was an

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.779
unbelievable rainstorm.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.599
Now, in the Pacific Northwest,

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.089
heat wave lit in Canada, 121

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:55.649
degrees, and next day it burned

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:56.650
down.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:04:59.729
Now there's an entire

00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:01.529
field of science called extreme

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.269
event attribution, where they can go

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.859
into these events and they can ask

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.839
the question, did climate change

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:07.840
play a role?

